  "opal": {
    "why": "opal's mocks are jest-mock's, and these tests check where @vitest/spy differs from it, as several of the tests' own comments say ('unlike jest'). (1) getMockImplementation: Vitest returns the implementation given to vi.fn(impl) and a pending mockImplementationOnce; jest-mock returns only what mockImplementation set ('implementation sync fn', 'returns temporary implementations'). (2) mockReset and mockRestore: Vitest goes back to vi.fn(impl)'s implementation or the spied original, while jest-mock leaves no implementation, so the call returns undefined ('.mockRestore() should restore initial implementation', 'docs example > mockReset', both 'mockReset should not restore original descriptor' tests). (3) Classes: Vitest calls a class implementation with new; jest-mock calls it as a function, which throws ('mock classes'), and doesn't throw on new for an arrow implementation ('spies on classes'). (4) Names: Vitest names a spy after its method, [MockFunction f] in the inline snapshot; jest-mock leaves it unnamed. (5) Disposal: jest-mock's Symbol.dispose restores without calling the mockRestore property, so a spy on mockRestore sees no call.",
